Q11.  Explain the purpose of the following image attributes:
 src
  alt
   width
    height 
Ans. The <img> tag in HTML uses attributes to specify the file location, accessibility text, and layout dimensions of an image.

src (Source): Specifies the path or URL to the image file you want to display. It is required; without it, the browser does not know which image to load. It supports relative paths (e.g., src="images/cat.jpg") or absolute web URLs (e.g., src="[https://example.com/cat.jpg](https://example.com/cat.jpg)").

alt (Alternative Text): Provides textual description of the image. It serves three main functions:

Accessibility: Screen readers read this text aloud for visually impaired users.

Fallback: Displays in the browser if the image file fails to load or is broken.

SEO: Search engine crawlers index alt text to understand the content and context of the image.

width & height (Dimensions): Define the intrinsic dimensions of the image in pixels (no px unit needed in HTML5, e.g., width="600" height="400").

Specifying both helps the browser reserve the exact space needed before the image file downloads, preventing Cumulative Layout Shift (CLS) where page content abruptly jumps around during loading.


