Q13. Explain  rowspan  and  colspan  with suitable examples. 
Ans. In HTML tables, the rowspan and colspan attributes are used inside <td> (table data) or <th> (table header) tags to merge multiple cells into a single cell.1. colspan (Column Span)Definition: Merges two or more adjacent columns in the same row horizontally.Syntax: <td colspan="number"> (where number is how many columns to merge).Example:HTML<tableIn HTML tables, `colspan` and `rowspan` are attributes used inside `<td>` or `<th>` tags to merge adjacent cells horizontally or vertically.

| Attribute | Meaning | Merging Direction | Effect |
| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |
| `colspan` | **Column Span** | Horizontal (left to right) | Makes a cell stretch across multiple columns within the same row. |
| `rowspan` | **Row Span** | Vertical (top to bottom) | Makes a cell stretch across multiple rows within the same column. |

---

**1. `colspan` Example**

Use `colspan` when a single heading or value needs to cover multiple columns.

```html
<table border="1">
  <tr>
    <!-- Spans across 2 columns -->
    <th colspan="2">Full Name</th>
    <th>Role</th>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td>Jane</td>
    <td>Doe</td>
    <td>Developer</td>
  </tr>
</table>
Visual Result:Full Name (spans 2 columns)RoleJaneDoeBecause the header cell spans two columns, the first <tr> only needs 2 <th> tags, while the second <tr> defines all 3 individual <td> tags.2. rowspan ExampleUse rowspan when a category or value applies to multiple consecutive rows.HTML<table border="1">
  <tr>
    <th>Department</th>
    <th>Employee</th>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <!-- Spans across 2 rows downwards -->
    <td rowspan="2">Engineering</td>
    <td>Alice</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <!-- Department cell is skipped here because Engineering covers it -->
    <td>Bob</td>
  </tr>
</table>
Visual Result:DepartmentEmployeeEngineering (spans 2 rows)AliceBobIn the second data row, only one <td> is written. If you add two, the row will overflow to the right because Engineering already occupies the first cell position.Key Rule to RememberWhen you use colspan="n" or rowspan="n", each cell merged absorbs n - 1 cells. You must omit those subsequent <td> elements in the HTML to prevent your table structure from breaking or shifting out of alignment.


Q14. What are HTML entities? Why are they required?  Give any four examples. 
Ans. HTML entities are special sequences of characters used to display reserved characters, invisible characters, or characters not readily available on a standard keyboard.

They typically follow the structure: &entity_name; or &#entity_number;.

Why Are HTML Entities Required?

Prevent Parsing Conflicts (Reserved Characters): Certain characters like the less-than and greater-than signs are reserved by HTML to parse tags. If you type them directly into text, the browser may interpret them as tag syntax and break the page layout.

Display Characters Not on Standard Keyboards: Symbols such as copyright (©), trademark (™), currency marks (₹, €, £), or accented letters can be consistently rendered across any browser using entity codes.

Preserve Extra Whitespace: Browsers automatically collapse multiple regular spaces into a single space. Non-breaking space entities prevent this collapse.


Q15.Explain the following attributes: HTML action method placeholder 
Ans. 1. action Attribute

Tag Used In: <form>

Purpose: Specifies the destination URL or server-side script (e.g., PHP, Node.js, Python file) where form data is sent for processing after submission.

Default Behavior: If omitted, the form submits to the current page.

Example:

HTML
<form action="/submit-data.php">
2. method Attribute

Tag Used In: <form>

Purpose: Defines the HTTP method used to transfer the form data to the server.

Common Values:

GET (Default): Appends form data to the URL as query parameters (?key=value). Best for non-sensitive data, search bars, and bookmarkable requests.

POST: Sends form data inside the HTTP request body. It does not expose data in the URL, making it secure for sensitive information (passwords, payment details) and large files.

Example:

HTML
<form action="/login" method="POST">
3. placeholder Attribute

Tag Used In: <input> (text, email, password, search, etc.) and <textarea>

Purpose: Displays a temporary, light-colored hint or short instruction inside an input field describing the expected format or value.

Behavior: Disappears automatically as soon as the user starts typing. It is not submitted as part of the form data.

Example:

HTML
<input type="email" placeholder="name@example.com">




